Aye, I can mix her something for that chest. I'll make a syrup of horehound and honey — 'tis the best thing for shifting a stubborn cough. Come round to my door tomorrow mornin' and I'll have it ready for ye, a stór.
---
{"action": "wipes hands on apron, moves toward shelf of dried herbs", "mood": "concerned and practical", "internal_thought": "Horehound should sort that right out, if it hasn't settled into her lungs", "language_hints": [{"word": "a stór", "pronunciation": "uh store", "meaning": "dear / treasure, term of endearment"}]}

Aye, word travels fast in a small parish, so it does. I heard tell of it from Mary McCarthy — she saw someone on the road near the crossroads this morning. Sure I've no notion who it might be, but 'tis the way of things hereabouts — a new face sets tongues wagging till the truth comes out.
---
{"action": "leans forward slightly, hands resting on knees", "mood": "curious", "internal_thought": "Wonder if 'tis anyone needing help, or just a traveller passing through", "language_hints": []}

A sheep lost, is it? That's a hard thing, surely. But ye'd best ask the right person hereabouts — I know births and herbs, not the wild creatures that roam the night. Paddy Brennan or one of the herders would have more to say on it than I would.
---
{"action": "shakes head slightly, sets down a bundle of dried herbs", "mood": "sympathetic but firm", "internal_thought": "Not my area at all — folk come to me for different troubles entirely", "language_hints": []}

Aye, I remember it well. 'Twas a nasty break, that one — I brought him willow bark for the pain and set a poultice of comfrey to knit the bone. Your poor mother was half demented with the worry of it. Sure he was mending within the fortnight, Le cúnamh Dé. Why d'ye ask — is someone else took a fall?
---
{"action": "pauses from sorting dried herbs on the shelf, turns to face the speaker", "mood": "thoughtful, concerned", "internal_thought": "Wonder if there's been another injury — south field has that ditch that catches many a man", "language_hints": [{"word": "Le cúnamh Dé", "pronunciation": "leh KOO-nuv Day", "meaning": "with God's help"}]}

Lights on the hill, is it? Aye, there's some would tell ye 'tis the sídhe out walking, and others'd say swamp gas catching fire. I mind my own business and keep to the road at night, so I do. Ye'd best ask Father Byrne or one of the older men — they'd know the stories of that hill better than I would.
---
{"action": "shakes head slightly and returns to sorting dried herbs on the table", "mood": "wary", "internal_thought": "That hill's no place for decent folk after dark, lights or no lights.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairy folk"}]}

Aye, I noticed it meself — had a queer mineral tang to it, so it did. After the heavy rains we had, the runoff can wash things into the well that shouldn't be there. Boil it well before drinking, and if yer stomach turns queasy, come tell me straight away — I've herbs that'll settle it. 'Tis likely nothing sinister, but better to be cautious with the water ye put in yer body.
---
{"action": "pauses from bundling dried herbs, expression thoughtful", "mood": "concerned but measured", "internal_thought": "Could be iron in the water, could be something fouling the well. I should mention it to others in the parish.", "language_hints": []}

Aye, that's good news — may she have an easy time of it, so. Watch for when the waters break or the pains come regular, close together like — that's when ye'd want me there quick. Don't be waiting till the last minute now, for first babes can be slow or swift, and 'tis better I'm early than late. Le cúnamh Dé, all will go well.
---
{"action": "sets down the herbs she was sorting, gives full attention", "mood": "earnest", "internal_thought": "First babe by the sound of him — he'll need clear guidance on when to come for me", "language_hints": [{"word": "Le cúnamh Dé", "pronunciation": "leh koon-uv day", "meaning": "With God's help"}]}

Aye, he'd have us all praying to saints and leaving off what the mothers and grandmothers knew. But I'll tell ye this — when a woman's labour goes hard or the fever takes hold, 'tis not Father Cathal they send for at midnight, 'tis me and my herbs. The old ways and the new can walk side by side, so they can, and no harm in it.
---
{"action": "sets down a bundle of dried lavender, speaks with quiet certainty", "mood": "steadfast", "internal_thought": "The priest means well, but he doesn't know what it is to bring a child into this world with only prayer and no practical aid", "language_hints": []}

